sub:Head {
  this: a np:Nanopublication ;
    np:hasAssertion sub:assertion ;
    np:hasProvenance sub:provenance ;
    np:hasPublicationInfo sub:pubinfo .
}

sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/scaling-strength-law> a delphi:Concept, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Strength scales as L^2: the missing half of Galileo's square-cube argument (R7) (Lean 4, kernel-checked)" ;
    dct:description "A member of cross-section A and breaking stress σ fails under axial load σ A; with isometric sections and the same material the breaking load scales as the square of the linear dimension, the safety factor falls as 1/L, and above σ k3/(k1 n) the member cannot carry n body weights. The missing half of Galileo's square-cube argument." ;
    dct:source "Proved in this project (lean/Delphi/Scaling.lean) from the physics thread's formal statements (physics/FORMAL-STATEMENTS.md, 2026-10-31; Galileo 1638 and Thompson 1917 ch. II for the idea); hypotheses named, no paper states it in this form." ;
    skos:notation "F_break = σ A ∝ L^2 ; F_break/W ∝ 1/L ; L_max = σ k3/(k1 n)" ;
    skos:definition """/-- **R7, conclusion 1.** Breaking load scales as `L²`. -/
theorem breakLoad_ratio {A : ℝ → ℝ} {k₃ σ : ℝ} (hA : AreaLaw A k₃) (hσ : 0 < σ) {L₁ L₂ : ℝ} (h₁ : 0 < L₁)
    (h₂ : 0 < L₂) : breakLoad σ A L₂ / breakLoad σ A L₁ = (L₂ / L₁) ^ 2"""^^delphi:lean4 ;
    schema:programmingLanguage "Lean 4" ;
    delphi:leanDeclaration "Delphi.breakLoad" ;
    delphi:leanTheorem "Delphi.breakLoad_ratio", "Delphi.safetyFactor_eq", "Delphi.safetyFactor_scaled", "Delphi.stress_ratio", "Delphi.cannot_carry" ;
    delphi:leanVerified "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    skos:scopeNote "Axial loading, isometric sections and constant σ are named hypotheses; real animals thicken bones with size (elastic similarity gives A ∝ L^2.5 under a different hypothesis)." ;

    rdfs:comment "Status: kernel-checked (Lean 4 accepted the module with zero sorry and standard axioms only). Not expert-validated. Does not establish: anything about organisms. The theorem is exact inside the stated model and silent outside it. The assumptions it rests on, written in the module header and never proved, are: Axial loading (bending has the same L^2 dependence with a different constant; not formalised); Isometric load-bearing sections and the same material (constant σ): real animals thicken bones with size; Nothing about any real giant: the checker rules only on a candidate that commits to isometry, the same material and a safety factor. Kernel-checked is not expert-validated: no human expert has validated these statements. Whether the Lean statement faithfully renders the source is a separate assessment record, and so far only one assessor provider has run." ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/fractastical/delphisci/blob/4ebb56add61de66744fe8b957ecd7c0bcecc8083/lean/Delphi/Scaling.lean>, <https://delphibio.org/t/lean/lean.json>, <https://delphibio.org/t/lean/> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/scaling-strength-law> dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/scaling-strength-law> ;
    delphi:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/lean-kernel-check-scaling-strength-law> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/lean-kernel-check-scaling-strength-law> a delphi:EvidenceItem ;
    rdfs:label "Lean 4 kernel accepted Delphi.breakLoad_ratio with zero sorry and standard axioms; it also rejected 2 deliberately broken variant(s) of this statement or its model" ;
    dct:description "Build receipt. Lean (version 4.35.0, x86_64-w64-windows-gnu, commit eb6cafb7290e8bd5f2937522d8d804f58f02bafc, Release); toolchain leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0; Mathlib v4.35.0 (56a005855ea17e0b0958f73ec801ae140b0a1679). lake build exit 0 in 5.3 s with 0 errors; 0 sorry in lean/Delphi/Scaling.lean (320 lines). #print axioms for Delphi.breakLoad_ratio, Delphi.safetyFactor_eq, Delphi.safetyFactor_scaled, Delphi.stress_ratio, Delphi.cannot_carry: Classical.choice, Quot.sound, propext only (Lean's standard axioms; no sorryAx). Mutation evidence (lean/mutate.mjs, 2026-10-31T22:57:06.367Z): 2 deliberate change(s) to this statement or its model were each rejected by the compiler: [scaling-safety-factor-constant] R7 mutant: safety factor claimed independent of L (σ k3 / k1), i.e. the 1/L fall removed. Rejected with 2 error(s): Scaling.lean:97:58: error: unsolved goals [scaling-strength-exponent-three] R7 mutant: breaking load claimed to scale as (L2/L1)^3 (then the safety factor would be constant and no L_max would exist). Rejected with 1 error(s): Scaling.lean:93:2: error: Type mismatch Controls: 17 of 17 unmodified modules accepted by the same command." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/scaling-strength-law> ;
    rdfs:comment "This evidence item establishes that the stated theorem follows from the stated model. Does not establish: anything about organisms. The theorem is exact inside the stated model and silent outside it. The assumptions it rests on, written in the module header and never proved, are: Axial loading (bending has the same L^2 dependence with a different constant; not formalised); Isometric load-bearing sections and the same material (constant σ): real animals thicken bones with size; Nothing about any real giant: the checker rules only on a candidate that commits to isometry, the same material and a safety factor. Kernel-checked is not expert-validated: no human expert has validated these statements. Whether the Lean statement faithfully renders the source is a separate assessment record, and so far only one assessor provider has run." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://delphibio.org/t/lean/lean.json>, <https://github.com/fractastical/delphisci/blob/4ebb56add61de66744fe8b957ecd7c0bcecc8083/lean/mutations.json>, <https://github.com/fractastical/delphisci/blob/4ebb56add61de66744fe8b957ecd7c0bcecc8083/lean/Delphi/Audit.lean> .
}

